  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 216 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A dying cold front will come through early this morning \nand bring a few light showers, but weak forcing and dry low levels \nwill minimize the impacts. Ridging will become more established and \ndominate the synoptic pattern going into Wednesday, continuing the \nquiet and relatively seasonable to above normal stretch. Overall, \nhighs will be in the 70s to lower 80s through Thursday. Lows tonight \nwill be in the mid 40s to lower 60s, increasing to the upper 50s to \nmid 60s Wednesday night with the arrival of a warm frontal boundary \nattached to a deepening low pressure system in Canada. While warm \nair advection will bring a more humid airmass into the region, there \nwill not be enough time for a fully tropical airmass to become \nestablished. Rain arrives throughout the day Wednesday in northern \nareas as a warm front pushes through, though it appears to lack much \nelevated instability, so the rain should just be stratiform. The \ncenter of the low will pass by well to the north of our area, and \nthe best dynamics should miss the region. Therefore, this will limit \ninstability as the system passes by. The highest instability will be \nacross the St. Lawrence Valley where the warmer airmass has the most \ntime to become established. Dew points there should briefly reach \nthe mid 60s to around 70 and a few rumbles of thunder are possible \nthere. The cold front passes through Wednesday night into early \nThursday, but the warm sector never looks to become fully \nestablished in the region, so most convection should remain to the \nsouthwest. St. Lawrence County and southern Vermont look to be the \nplaces that may see more convective enhancement, while the rest of \nthe region should mostly see synoptically forced rain. However, if \nthe trend of earlier precipitation and a faster system continues, we \nmay see more availability of instability on Wednesday for more \nwidespread thunderstorms. A strong southwesterly low-level jet will \nreach the 40-55 KT range at 850 mb Wednesday and Wednesday night, \ncausing downsloping in the typical spots like the Champlain Valley, \nwhich could limit rainfall totals. Overall expected totals range in \nthe 0.15 to 0.75 inch range for most places, with some higher totals \nlocally along the spine of the Green Mountains and Adirondacks. This \njet will also lead to widespread gusts in the 20-30 mph range, with \nlocally higher gusts, particularly on summits. Then Thursday, winds \nshould stay elevated in cold air advection out of the west and \nnorthwest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure will build into the region towards the end \nof the week into the weekend, bringing a stretch of dry and pleasant \nweather. Daytime high temperatures on Friday will generally be in \nthe upper 60s and low 70s, with warmer temperatures expected for \nSaturday and Sunday in the 70s to near 80 as the ridging shifts \neastward leading to southerly flow across the region. Ideal \nradiational cooling conditions are expected, especially Friday night \ninto Saturday morning with the high pressure overhead. Overnight \nlows are expected to drop into the 40s, with some of the cold \nhollows dropping into the mid/upper 30s while locations near Lake \nChamplain will stay in the 50s. While dry weather will persist \nthrough much of the weekend, precipitation chances will increase \nthrough the day on Sunday with a shortwave trough pushing through, \nalthough there is still plenty of uncertainty regarding this feature.",
